// The declaration is verified per instance: `Msg<Shared<Leaf>>` declares `Sendable` (the template does,
// unconditionally), but its `Data` payload is a `Shared` over a mutable type, so the claim is false and
// refused at the declaration, naming the payload.
import { std::concurrent::Channel, std::memory::Shared };
type value Leaf { public int32 v; public ctor make(int32 v) { this.v = v; } }
type enum Msg<T> implements Sendable { Data(T v), Stop }
fn int32 main() { Channel<Msg<Shared<Leaf>>> bad = Channel.bounded(capacity: 1); return 0; }
